mido: write the console ring from the console path, not a thread (0145)

0143 started the flusher in a kernel thread created from mido_sdhci_init(), and
two boots later the SYSTEM partition was still all zeroes.  The thread is the
problem: mido_sdhci_init() runs from IOKitBSDInit, well before the thread
subsystem is usable, so kernel_thread_start() failed and the ring was discarded
at the next reset.  Nothing in the kernel noticed, because a failed start only
meant "no log".

Drive the write from the console instead.  sweet_log_append() (0047) now calls
mido_log_pump() after every append, and the pump writes whatever has piled up
once 4 KiB is buffered -- no thread, no startup ordering, and the early lines
before the eMMC exists are simply skipped because se.log_ok is still false.

The pump is a weak symbol in 0047 with an empty default, so the UFS boards keep
their thread and polaris links unchanged.

Panic paths are excluded: an SDHCI write is polled with multi-second timeouts,
and blocking a panic on a dead card would lose the panic text rather than save
it.

The header and layout are unchanged from 0143, and so is tools/mido-log-pull.py.
--- a/bsd/dev/mido_sdhci.c
